{
 "lead": "Two more diode circuits complete the uncontrolled family. A voltage doubler uses two diodes and two capacitors to give about twice the supply peak with no step-up transformer, as in small high-voltage supplies and 115/230 V selectable supplies. A three-phase diode bridge takes all three phases and gives a stiff DC with only 4 % ripple; it is the front end of almost every industrial drive, welder and large battery charger.",
 "prereq": "You need: the capacitor filter and its ripple Idc/(fC) from <a href=\"#capacitor-and-lc-filters-for-rectifiers\">capacitor and LC filters</a>, and the bridge PIV argument from <a href=\"#diode-rectifiers-half-wave-full-wave-and-bridge\">diode rectifiers</a>.",
 "sections": [
  {
   "h3": "Voltage doublers",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "The <b>full-wave (bridge) doubler</b> puts two equal capacitors C1 and C2 in series across the load, with their midpoint joined to one supply terminal. On the positive half-cycle D1 charges C1 to Vm; on the negative half-cycle D2 charges C2 to Vm. The load sees the two in series:"
    },
    {
     "type": "formula",
     "text": "Vo(no load) = 2Vm;   ΔVpp ≈ Idc/(fC);   Vdc ≈ 2Vm − Idc/(fC)",
     "where": "Vm is the peak supply voltage in V, Idc the load current in A, f the supply frequency in Hz and C the value of each capacitor in F. Each capacitor is recharged once per cycle but the output is topped up twice, so the ripple is at 2f."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Why Idc/(fC)? Each capacitor carries the full load current for a whole cycle T = 1/f before it is recharged, so each loses Idc·T/C. The output dips by that amount in the half-cycle when neither is charging, and on average sits one full ΔV below 2Vm. Each diode blocks 2Vm; each capacitor needs a Vm rating."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "The <b>half-wave doubler</b> (a clamp followed by a peak rectifier) puts C1 in series with the supply. On the negative half-cycle D1 charges C1 to Vm; on the positive half-cycle the supply and C1 add, so D2 charges C2 to 2Vm. One output terminal is common with the supply, but C2 is topped up only once per cycle, so the ripple is at f and the output falls faster with load. C1 needs a Vm rating, C2 and both diodes 2Vm. Repeated stages make a voltage multiplier for kilovolt supplies at microamps."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "h3": "Three-phase diode bridge",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Six diodes, two per phase: D1, D3, D5 have their cathodes joined to DC+, and D4, D6, D2 their anodes joined to DC−. At every instant the upper diode on the most positive phase and the lower diode on the most negative phase conduct, so the load sees the largest line-to-line voltage. The pair changes every 60° in the order D1D2, D2D3, D3D4, D4D5, D5D6, D6D1, and each diode conducts for 120°."
    },
    {
     "type": "formula",
     "text": "Vdc = (3/π) VLm = (3√2/π) VL = 1.35 VL",
     "where": "VL is the line-to-line RMS voltage and VLm = √2 VL its peak, in V. The average is taken over one 60° cap of a line voltage: (3/π)∫ VLm sin θ dθ from 60° to 120°."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "The output swings between VLm (the top of each cap) and VLm cos 30° = 0.866 VLm, six times per cycle. Its RMS is 0.9558 VLm, so FF = 1.0009 and RF = 0.042, that is 4.2 %, against 48 % for a single-phase bridge. The lowest ripple frequency is 6f = 300 Hz on a 50 Hz supply. Each diode blocks the line peak, PIV = VLm, and the TUF is 0.955."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "With a large DC inductor the load current is a flat Id. Each line then carries a quasi-square current: +Id for 120°, zero for 60°, −Id for 120°, zero for 60°. Its RMS is √(2/3) Id = 0.816 Id and its fundamental RMS is (√6/π) Id = 0.780 Id, so the distortion factor is 3/π = 0.955 and the harmonics present are 5, 7, 11, 13 and so on (more in <a href=\"#performance-displacement-factor-ripple-and-harmonics\">performance</a>)."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "The <b>three-phase half-wave (three-pulse)</b> diode circuit has one diode per phase and returns the load to the neutral: Vdc = (3√3/2π) Vm = 1.17 Vph (Vm the phase peak, Vph the phase RMS), with ripple at 3f and DC in the transformer windings."
    },
    {
     "type": "table",
     "head": [
      "Circuit",
      "Diodes",
      "Vdc (no filter)",
      "Ripple",
      "PIV"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "Full-wave doubler",
       "2 (+2 C)",
       "2Vm at no load",
       "2f",
       "2Vm"
      ],
      [
       "Half-wave doubler",
       "2 (+2 C)",
       "2Vm at no load",
       "f",
       "2Vm"
      ],
      [
       "3-phase half-wave",
       "3",
       "1.17 Vph",
       "3f",
       "√3 Vm"
      ],
      [
       "3-phase bridge",
       "6",
       "1.35 VL",
       "6f",
       "VLm = √2 VL"
      ]
     ]
    }
   ]
  }
 ],
 "examples": [
  {
   "question": "A full-wave voltage doubler runs from a 12 V rms, 50 Hz transformer secondary with two 1000 µF capacitors and supplies 50 mA. Take ideal diodes. Find the no-load output, the ripple and the loaded average output, and the diode PIV.",
   "steps": [
    "Vm = √2 × 12 = 16.97 V, so the no-load output is 2Vm = 33.94 V.",
    "Each capacitor loses Idc/(fC) = 0.05 / (50 × 1000 × 10⁻⁶) = 1.00 V per cycle, so the output ripple is about 1.0 V peak to peak, at 2f = 100 Hz.",
    "Vdc ≈ 2Vm − Idc/(fC) = 33.94 − 1.00 = 32.94 V.",
    "A step-by-step simulation of the circuit gives 33.0 V average and 0.9 V ripple: the estimate is good.",
    "PIV of each diode = 2Vm = 33.94 V; each capacitor sees Vm = 16.97 V."
   ],
   "answer": "33.94 V at no load; with 50 mA about 32.9 V with 1.0 V of 100 Hz ripple; PIV 33.9 V."
  },
  {
   "question": "A three-phase diode bridge is fed from a 415 V, 50 Hz line and supplies R = 20 Ω through a large smoothing inductor. Find Vdc, Id, the diode average and RMS currents, the PIV, the line RMS current and the input power factor.",
   "steps": [
    "VLm = √2 × 415 = 586.90 V. Vdc = 3VLm/π = 3 × 586.90 / 3.1416 = 560.45 V.",
    "Id = Vdc/R = 560.45/20 = 28.02 A (the inductor drops no average voltage).",
    "Each diode conducts 120° of 360°: I(avg) = Id/3 = 9.34 A, I(rms) = Id/√3 = 16.18 A. PIV = VLm = 586.9 V.",
    "Line RMS current = √(2/3) × 28.02 = 22.88 A; fundamental = (√6/π) × 28.02 = 21.85 A.",
    "Power P = Vdc Id = 560.45 × 28.02 = 15.71 kW. Apparent power S = √3 × 415 × 22.88 = 16.45 kVA.",
    "PF = P/S = 15.71/16.45 = 0.955 = 3/π. The fundamental is in phase with the voltage, so all of the shortfall is distortion."
   ],
   "answer": "Vdc = 560.4 V, Id = 28.0 A, diode 9.34 A average and 16.18 A RMS, PIV 586.9 V, line current 22.88 A RMS, PF 0.955."
  }
 ],
 "traps": [
  "Giving a voltage doubler 2Vm under load. 2Vm is the no-load value; every milliamp of load pulls it down by about 1/(fC) volts per ampere.",
  "Putting the half-wave doubler's ripple at 2f. Its output capacitor is topped up once per cycle, so its ripple is at f; only the full-wave doubler gives 2f.",
  "Using the phase voltage in Vdc = 1.35 VL. The 1.35 form needs line RMS; with phase RMS it is 2.34 Vph.",
  "Saying each diode of a three-phase bridge conducts for 60°. Each conducts 120°; the conducting pair changes every 60°.",
  "Taking the three-phase bridge PIV as the phase peak. Each diode blocks the line-to-line peak, √2 VL."
 ],
 "quiz": [
